Hi Annunci,

I would recommend you to check on this amazing post  <https://psu-psychology.github.io/psy-597-SEM/10_invariance_groups/invariance_groups_constraints.html#equality-constraints-in-sem>by Michael Hallquist on this issue. On your second question, question, it says:

"Circling back to our failure to corroborate strong invariance, we might wish to look at which constraints on the intercepts are leading to the greatest reduction in fit. Whereas the modificationIndices in lavaan gives information on change in model χ2if we add additional parameters, we need to use the lavTestScore function to examine how model χ2 would improve if we freed (removed) these constraints."

Now, on your particular questions:

To find the modification indices who advise me to remove the intercept of my item do I need to work on my metric invariance, right? Or on my scalar or partial strong model?

***I am not sure what you mean here. Metric (weak) invariance means constraining the loadings to be equal across groups, and allowing intercepts and means to be freely estimated. The intercepts are then fixed in scalar (strong) invariance. If the model has a bad change of fit (CFI delta >= .01), then you can use lavTestScore to find which in which group could the intercepts be freed to have a better fit. If you free the intercepts for some groups but they remain fixed for others, then you achieved partial strong invariance (if the CFI delta is now < .01).

Does this answer your questions?

Best,

Alejandro

